Once I began lurking in this forum, I decided to switch over to ascii to get 
The Genuine NetHack Experience. I played for a short while that way, then 
switched back. It just seemed to be too much hassle to learn what all the 
symbols meant, especially when there were more ambiguities (e.g., spellbooks 
and doors) which were not there in the graphical version.

People always said that the graphical version looked silly, that their 
imaginations made, for example, a group of L much more frightening than any 
tile could look, etc. What that ignores is that after some time of play, the 
player isn't really paying attention to the symbol/tile any more at all. 
He's seeing *through* the screen and into the game itself. It doesn't matter 
that characters look in the wrong direction and arrows fly sideways. You're 
focused on playing the game; the interface is just a means to that end.

So far as I am concerned, the advantages of ascii are knowing intuitively 
what species go together for blessed genocide, and a certain "retro" 
quality. The advantage of tiles is a more intuitive interface. And that's 
it. Everything else regarding interface preference is strictly habitual.

